## Model details

Random Forest, Extra Trees, and CatBoost models use five-fold `GroupKFold` validation
on the training portion, with each molecule-solvent pair kept together. They report mean and
standard deviation across folds, then evaluate once on a separate test split.
The `rfg_basic.py`, `etr_basic.py`, and `catboost_basic.py` variants keep a
row-wise test split for comparison; the other variants use a molecule-solvent-pair-grouped
test split.

The models use:

- `RandomForestRegressor`
- `ExtraTreesRegressor` as a parallel implementation for each data/split variant
- `CatBoostRegressor` with native solvent categorical handling, 400 boosting iterations, learning rate 0.05, and depth 8
- the same primary hyperparameters, including 400 trees and `max_features=0.5`
- five-fold `GroupKFold` validation that keeps each molecule-solvent pair together
- a final model refit on the full non-test data before holdout evaluation
- row-wise test splits for the `*_basic.py` variants; molecule-solvent-pair-grouped test splits for the other variants
- validation permutation importance as descriptive feature importance, not a significance test
- metrics: MAE, RMSE, and R²

The parser stores a canonical `solvent_id` alongside each row so pair grouping
uses the actual solvent molecule. Re-run `python src/dataparser.py` to add this
column to existing processed datasets; solvent-descriptor and all-entry models
require it because descriptor vectors alone do not uniquely identify every
solvent. Basic datasets can use the solvent name when `solvent_id` is absent.
The Random Forest and Extra Trees basic models one-hot encode the solvent name;
CatBoost handles it natively as categorical data. Solvent-descriptor models use
numeric solvent descriptors instead. The all-entries models use rows with valid
`logS` values without applying the solvent whitelist used for `data_sol_desc.csv`.

## Notes

- The project is intended as a research/practical machine learning workflow for solubility prediction.
- The descriptor extraction is designed around RDKit molecule calculations and is suitable for exploring structure-property relationships.
- The repository is still structured as a research prototype and can be extended with alternative models, hyperparameter tuning, or a more comprehensive validation pipeline.

